<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1982">1982</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as UNCLOS</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) is an international treaty adopted in 1982 after nine years of negotiation and in force since 1994. It defines a 12-nautical-mile territorial sea, a 200-mile exclusive economic zone and rights over the continental shelf, and recognises archipelagic states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. It also created the International Tribunal for the Law of the Sea and the International Seabed Authority.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-nations-convention-on-the-law-of-the-sea.jpg" alt="United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UNCLOS_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Convention replaced the older freedom of the seas, dating from the 17th century. National rights were limited to a belt of water, usually three nautical miles, and all waters beyond were open to every nation, the mare liberum principle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-grotius">Hugo Grotius</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> conference at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> in 1930 reached no agreement, and in 1945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-s-truman">Harry S. Truman</a> extended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> control to all the natural resources of its continental shelf. Between 1946 and 1950 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> claimed 200 nautical miles to cover their Humboldt Current fishing grounds.</p><p>The first United Nations Conference, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> in 1958, produced four treaties but left open the breadth of territorial waters. Arvid Pardo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> raised the varying claims at the United Nations in 1967, and the Third Conference convened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1973. It used consensus rather than majority voting, with more than 160 nations taking part, and lasted until 1982. The Convention came into force on 16 November 1994, a year after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyana">Guyana</a> became the 60th state to ratify it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Zones are measured from a baseline, normally the low-water line. The territorial sea extends 12 nautical miles, where the coastal state has sovereign rights, but ships have a right of innocent passage, which excludes fishing, polluting, weapons practice and spying. A contiguous zone reaches a further 12 nautical miles, where a state may enforce customs, taxation, immigration and pollution laws. Exclusive economic zones extend 200 nautical miles, within which the coastal state has sole rights over natural resources; they were introduced to halt clashes over fishing rights, though oil was also becoming important. Archipelagic states may draw baselines between their outermost islands.</p><p>Waters beyond these areas are the high seas, and the seabed there is called the Area. Part XI created the International Seabed Authority to authorise seabed exploration and mining, under the principle of the common heritage of mankind, and Part XII places duties on states to protect the marine environment. The Convention established the International Tribunal for the Law of the Sea in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> and gives landlocked states a right of access to the sea. It does not settle territorial disputes or issues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Convention codifies the principles of international maritime law, which rest on centuries of maritime experience, and strengthens and expands much of the 1958 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> law. A number of states signed but did not ratify it, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and others did not sign. In 2023 agreement was reached on the High Seas Treaty, an instrument under the Convention for conserving marine biodiversity in international waters.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Djuanda Declaration of 1957 asserted the archipelagic principle, later recognised in the 1982 <b>United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
